---
import Button from '@/components/ui/Button.astro';
import Section from '@/components/ui/Section.astro';
import { site } from '@/config/site';
import PageLayout from '@/layouts/PageLayout.astro';

const colophon = [
  { term: 'Framework', detail: 'Astro, built to static HTML' },
  { term: 'Content', detail: 'Markdown and MDX' },
  { term: 'Figures', detail: 'SVG and React, with D3 and Three.js to come' },
  { term: 'Typefaces', detail: 'Newsreader, Instrument Sans and JetBrains Mono' },
  { term: 'Hosting', detail: 'Cloudflare' },
];
---

<PageLayout section="about">
  <Section
    id="premise"
    label="Premise"
    title="Most explanations of AI ask you to take the picture on trust."
  >
    <div class="max-w-2xl space-y-5 text-lede text-ink-muted">
      <p>
        The ideas behind modern AI are visual and mechanical: surfaces being descended, vectors
        being rotated, attention moving across a sentence. The way through them is one path,
        <a href="/learning-paths#from-data-to-intelligence" class="link-underline text-ink hover:text-accent">From Data to Intelligence</a>,
        from a sample to an agent. Text alone is a poor medium for that walk, so the
        path points at a figure whenever the figure exists.
      </p>
      <p>
        This notebook explains those ideas the way they are easiest to understand — with figures
        you can manipulate, code you can run, and paths that put things in a sensible order.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="teaching" label="Teaching" title="How a page is supposed to teach.">
    <div class="max-w-2xl space-y-5 text-lede text-ink-muted">
      <p>
        Visual first. If an idea has a shape, the shape is the explanation and the paragraph is
        the caption. A loss, a boundary, a gradient, and a pattern of attention are objects. The
        lab exists so those objects can be changed, and so the change is the lesson. A definition
        that arrives before the picture asks the reader to trust a word.
      </p>
      <p>
        Structured, in the strong sense. A path is a sequence of dependencies. Each module says
        what you should be able to do at the end of it, which ideas that ability depends on, why
        those ideas are the ones that matter, and what the next module will assume. The order is
        the teaching. A list of related topics is not.
      </p>
      <p>
        Interactive where interaction carries a belief, and research-oriented everywhere else. A
        control that does not change what you think is decoration. A research entry either points
        at a source or states a question it has not answered. Notes are published when they are
        ready to be argued with, marked with their maturity, and revised in place. Code accompanies
        a claim that can be run, and it is the same code the figure uses.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="author" label="Author" title={site.name}>
    <div class="grid items-start gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[11rem_1fr]">
      <div class="flex aspect-[4/5] w-40 items-end border border-line bg-surface p-4 lg:w-full">
        <p class="mono-label text-ink-faint">[PERSONAL CONTENT NEEDED] Portrait</p>
      </div>
      <div class="max-w-2xl space-y-5 text-lede text-ink-muted">
      <p>
        {site.name} is a {site.role} at {site.employer} in {site.location}. He works on document
        understanding for Basecone, a product used in the Netherlands and Belgium: splitting,
        reading and checking the invoices and receipts that pass through it.
      </p>
      <p>
        He trained as a physicist. A BSc at Fergusson College was followed by an MSc in quantum
        mechanics at Savitribai Phule Pune University, and by research in computational physics at
        the university’s Center for Modeling and Simulation. The work since has been applied
        machine learning — industrial sound at Asquared IoT, telematics at KPIT, language and
        platform models at Exadatum and Fractal, and client systems at Accenture.
      </p>
      <p>
        He has taught physics to grades 11 and 12, and has served as visiting faculty and as a
        member of the Board of Studies at MIT-WPU for its Big Data and AI programmes. This
        notebook is where those ideas are written down so they can be seen, not only used.
      </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
      <Button href="/resume" variant="secondary" arrow>Résumé</Button>
      <Button href={`mailto:${site.email}`} variant="secondary">{site.email}</Button>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="colophon" label="Colophon" title="How this site is made.">
    <dl class="max-w-2xl border-t border-line">
      {
        colophon.map((item) => (
          <div class="grid gap-x-8 gap-y-1 border-b border-line py-4 sm:grid-cols-[9rem_1fr]">
            <dt class="mono-label pt-1 text-ink-faint">{item.term}</dt>
            <dd class="text-ink-muted">{item.detail}</dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>
  </Section>
</PageLayout>
